==============  HTTP Methods  ===============
idempotent :- "Making the same request once or multiple times has the same effect on the server."

Get
>> Retrieve a resource or a collection of resources.
>> Idempotent :- same effect : yes | safe : yes
ex. 200 success , 404 npt found

Post
>> Create a new resource.
>> Idempotent: ❌ No (Multiple calls may create duplicates with new id)
ex. 201 Created (Success + Location header) , 400 400 Bad Request (Invalid data)

Put
>> (Replace)  Replace an entire resource (full update)
>> Idempotent:  ✅ Yes (Repeating the same request has no further effect).
>>  expects the complete representation of the resource.
>> You must send all fields, even if only one field is modified
>> condition:- resource should exist |
>> It depends on the API design | ** if not - it will not replace, but create new resource
ex.
If the resource exists → Full update (HTTP 200 or 204).
If the resource doesn’t exist → Creation (HTTP 201).
If creation is disabled → 404 Not Found.

Patch
>> (Partial Update) update some part of resource
>> When PATCH is Not Idempotent (Default Behavior)
PATCH sends partial updates, and repeated requests may produce different results if the resource state changes between calls.
>> When PATCH is  Idempotent
?? can we replace whole resource | Yes
ex. 200 OK or 204 No Content (Success) , 400 Bad Request (Invalid partial data)

Delete
>> Delete a resource.
>> Idempotent:  ✅ Yes (Even if the resource is already deleted).
ex. 204 No Content (Success) , 404 Not Found (Resource doesn’t exist)

Head
>> Fetch Meta data | Retrieve only headers (no body). Useful for checking if a resource exists.
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
Content-Length: 124
ETag: "abc123"
Last-Modified: Mon, 15 Jul 2024 12:00:00 GMT

Options
>>  List supported HTTP methods for a resource.
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Allow: GET, POST, HEAD, OPTIONS
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST
