What happens when a user hits a URL?
Stepwise explanation:
  User enters URL in browser (e.g. https://www.example.com).
Browser parses the URL to extract:
   Protocol: HTTPS
   Domain Name: www.example.com
   Path: / (home page)
   Query parameters (if any).
DNS (Domain Name System)  Resolution: (return ip address of server)
>> dns maps domain names ↔ IP addresses
>> return ip address of server
>> It is the IP address of the server where the application is hosted
Browser checks cache (browser, OS, router) for IP address.
  If not found, sends request to DNS server to resolve domain name → IP address.
TCP Connection:
  Browser initiates a TCP connection to the resolved IP on port 443 (HTTPS) using the TCP three-way handshake.
TLS Handshake (if HTTPS):
  Browser and server establish a secure encrypted connection using SSL/TLS.
HTTP Request Sent:
  Browser sends an HTTP GET request to the server for the resource /.
Server Processes Request:
  Web server receives request, routes it to appropriate handler (static file, backend service, or application logic).
Server Responds:
  Sends back an HTTP response with status code, headers, and body (e.g. HTML content).
Browser Renders Response:
  Parses HTML, makes further requests for CSS, JS, images.
Renders the final page for the user.
